\import{base-macros}

\p{Trade-offs that were decided on purpose. A reviewing agent should not report these again; if the situation changed, open a problem tree that links here.}

\subtree[accepted-risks-divergence]{
\title{Where the default configuration may differ from the official checker}

\p{The maintainer chose practical exactness on 2026-11-24; see [standing policies](standing-policies). The default configuration must never accept a statement that is not derivable in Lean's type theory (\em{kind F}) and must give the official verdict on every real input. It may accept a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through its own incompleteness, non-transitivity, cache order or object identity (\em{kind T}). The record of the known classes, with one export for each, is [the record of the divergence classes](rep-official-divergence); the adversarial reviews behind it are [the adversarial review of the default set](rep-soundness-default-set) and [the audit of the reference engine's caches](rep-reference-caches). The short notes on them: [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract), [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence) and [the caches of the reference engine](design-reference-caches). Since the evening of 2026-11-24 the default configuration is the machine with \code{eager} and the two proof-comparison shortcuts, see [the decision](dec-YP0N), so the classes below are what a plain run of the executable does. Ground truth for every verdict there is the official checker of Lean v4.36.0 run on the export. No class of kind F is known.}

\p{\strong{The common root.} The official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} is not transitive and not a function of the pair it is asked about. With \code{\verb~|A = True /\ True~}, an axiom \code{\verb~|h : A~} and \code{\verb~|d x := And.rec (fun _ _ => 5) x~}, the kernel accepts \code{\verb~|d intro = d h~} and \code{\verb~|d intro = 5~} and rejects \code{\verb~|d h = 5~}. One of its caches is read by cheap reduction calls and written only by full ones, so the answer for a pair depends on what was reduced before; and one test is pointer equality, so the answer depends on whether a subterm is one export record or two. An engine that computes on values and keys its memo tables by values answers the question of the theory, which is transitive.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Stand-in types of terms the kernel has not reduced.} The official kernel types a bound variable by its binder's declared type. The machine binds a variable to a value and can use a type that is only convertible. Six of the seven shapes found (findings E1-F1 to E1-F6) now get the official verdict or a decline; the shape with convertible domains under the expected-type rule is accepted only with the switch \code{convexpect}, which is off. The seventh (E1-F7: the type of the structure of a stuck projection, and the raw major premise of the K rule) is still accepted, and is intrinsic to a machine on values. An exact treatment is described in the document and was not built.}
\li{\strong{A pair table keyed by values.} The machine's table of established comparisons is keyed by values; the official caches are keyed by terms and live for one declaration. The machine answers a comparison that the official kernel asks again for a new pair of terms (finding S3-F1; present in every switch set).}
\li{\strong{Memo that survives a declaration.} The switches \code{warm} and \code{pchk} keep results across declarations; officially every cache lives for one declaration (findings S3-F3 and S3-F5). The warm machine alone can also reject a valid unit; the pipeline's strict re-check turned that into the official accept until 2026-11-25. Since then a plain run reports the rejection, see [the known wrong rejects](accepted-risks-wrong-rejects).}
\li{\strong{One-sided unfolding as one reduction.} The switch \code{lazyeval} runs a one-sided unfolding against a weak-head-normal side as one reduction to weak-head normal form (finding S3-F4).}
\li{\strong{Metadata is transparent.} The loader drops metadata nodes in axioms, definitions, theorems and opaques, and the wrapped expression stands for the node; the official kernel treats the node as a term of its own (finding S3-F2). Declining such declarations belongs to the strict mode. Metadata inside an inductive or quotient record is declined in both modes.}
\li{\strong{Object identity of export records.} Two exports with the same terms, differing by one duplicated record, can get different official verdicts. Any engine over a hash-consed store accepts both (finding R2-F2). The strict mode of the reference engine declines where it would need the official object identity.}
\li{\strong{The two proof-comparison shortcuts}, in the default set since the evening of 2026-11-24. They do not compare proof arguments and try the arguments of one head before unfolding it; the official comparison is not a congruence where a reduction inspects a proof, so they accept statements it rejects. Each such statement is derivable; for 15 of the known exports the official checker accepts a proof of the same statement with the congruence step written out. With \code{eager} they also accept the hostile rows of the eager-reduction family. See [the shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts).}
\li{\strong{A repeat of a tolerated accept.} Since 2026-11-25 the default configuration does not check a definition, theorem or opaque again that repeats an accepted earlier one word for word: the same kind, the same universe-parameter names, the same reducibility hint and the same type and value records of the export. On one hostile file, \code{R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad}, that turns the official verdict into a tolerated accept. The file is the export \code{e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad} of the stand-in types (finding E1-F7) with its theorem repeated; the official checker rejects it, and the statement is derivable. The machine accepts the original, which is the tolerated accept of the first item, and rejects the same two records when it checks them a second time, so before the change the default configuration rejected the file at the repeat, as the official checker does; now it accepts it. Nothing underivable is accepted: the statement of a repeat is the statement of its original. Under the strict policy the file is rejected as officially, and \code{\verb~|-X repeats=check~} gives the earlier behaviour. Taken under the practical policy by [the decision of the second wave](dec-RHRE); the evidence is [the report](rep-OPT-I), section 3.5, where it is the only verdict that differs on 1,648 hostile files judged by the official checker. See [the accept of a repeat](design-verdict-protocol-repeats).}
\li{\strong{How many.} Through the launcher of the default configuration, 74 of the 1,274 rows of the hostile suite are accepts of this tolerated kind; no row is a wrong accept, 4 are declined, and 12 are the known wrong rejects of the next section. 94 rows carry the mark of the tolerated kind. These are the counts since the change of 2026-11-25 that made a rejection of the machine final. Before it, on the same 1,274 rows, 78 were tolerated accepts and no row was a wrong reject: the re-check of a rejection, which a plain run no longer makes, had turned four official rejections into accepts through its strict machine. Earlier still, before 78 rows were added on 2026-11-25, 11 of them marked and 10 of those accepted, the counts were 68 of 1,196 with 83 marked. The file of the item above is not a row of the suite. The mark is given by a reviewer, with a certificate that the official checker accepts or with an argument for a family of rows; that evidence is not a proof, see [what is left open](prob-HC9J).}
}

\p{\strong{Not tolerated.} The switch \code{k2} produced a wrong accept with its default thresholds (finding S3-F6) and stays off. A declaration that enters eager reduction is declined by the machine without the switch \code{eager} and decided by the reference engine; with the switch by itself, no divergence is known. And a rejection of what the official checker accepts was never tolerated, also on a hand-made file; \strong{since 2026-11-25 this sentence has twelve known exceptions in the default configuration}, which the maintainer's instruction of that day accepts until the machine is aligned, see [the known wrong rejects](accepted-risks-wrong-rejects). It stays the rule for every real input and the goal for the machine on any file. Two such cases were found on 2026-11-24, both through a metadata node that the loader drops: the node can hide \code{eagerReduce} from a syntactic test of the official kernel, which then compares plainly and accepts, while an engine that sees through the node enters eager reduction and rejects; and the node can stop the official cheap beta reduction, so that the official cache answers a comparison that fails without the node, see [the example](ex-FD6K). Both are closed since that evening: the loader declines a declaration with a node around an application of \code{eagerReduce}, and the launcher checked every practical rejection of a file with a dropped metadata node again under the strict policy and reported that verdict, see [the decision](dec-7UAL). Since 2026-11-25 that second run is made only with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} and where the reference engine is the primary engine; for a plain run this opens nothing known, because the machine accepts the four files of the example by itself. The sentence that stood here until then, "No rejection of an official accept is known in the default configuration", is superseded by the next section.}

\p{\strong{What the gates show for real inputs}, as recorded in the divergence document for the machine with the fix of the stand-in types: no wrong verdict in the full gate of the plain machine and of the default switch set; 17,775 mutants with no soundness and no completeness difference; no verdict difference from the reference engine on 1,153,493 engine checks over the 230 arena tests, Init, Std, cedar, cslib, reachability, a slice of Cedar and 16 slices of Mathlib. The adversarial review of the candidate default found no kind F on 987 hostile exports with official verdicts. With that candidate as the default set and the machine as default engine, the gate of the default configuration through the launcher shows, on the evening of 2026-11-24: 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants with no soundness and no completeness difference, 1,196 hostile rows with 68 tolerated accepts, 4 declines and no wrong verdict, and no difference of the differential engine on 1,141,195 checks. On 2026-11-25, with the changes of the second optimisation wave: 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants with no soundness and no completeness difference, the 1,274 hostile rows with 78 tolerated accepts, 4 declines and no wrong verdict, through the executable and through the jars, see [the integration of the second wave](rep-INT9). Since the change of that evening, with a rejection of the machine final: 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants with no soundness and no completeness difference (16,092 in agreement, 1,683 declined), the 1,274 hostile rows as counted above, and the eight corpora and the 270 arena tests with the verdicts of before, see [the census](rep-MO-1).}
}

\subtree[accepted-risks-wrong-rejects]{
\title{Known wrong rejects of the default configuration on hand-made files}

\p{Since 2026-11-25 (\commit{a8dc6dd}) the default configuration rejects twelve hand-made exports that the official checker accepts. This is accepted on purpose and for a time. The maintainer's instruction of that day is that the reference engine is a tool of development that shall not affect production, and that the machine is to be aligned with the official checker itself; it is the item "The machine is the whole checker in production" of the [standing policies](standing-policies-conduct), and what was decided on it is [the decision](dec-PUU0). Until then the reference engine confirmed every rejection of the machine, and that re-check repaired exactly these twelve rows. A reviewing agent should not report the twelve again. A thirteenth row of the suite, a wrong reject on real input (anything an exporter writes, an arena test, a mutant) and any accept of a statement that is not derivable are findings and are reported at once.}

\p{\strong{Why it is acceptable.} The re-check changed no verdict on real input: the machine rejects no unit of the eight corpora, and of its 1,960 rejections on the 262 small arena tests and the 17,775 mutants the re-check changed none. Turning it off can only turn an accept into a rejection or a decline, so it cannot add an accept of a statement that is not derivable, and on 19,319 files it added none. The twelve are hand-made files of two known classes, written by the reviews and packages of the project to show where the machine's memo differs from the official caches. This is a debt, not a tolerated class: the contract tolerates an accept of kind T, and it never tolerated a rejection of an official accept.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Cause}\th{Rows}\th{What makes the machine reject}\th{Who accepts}\th{What closes them}}
\tr{\td{A memo that survives a declaration: the switch \code{warm}, the memo class of [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence-memo), finding S3-F3}\td{8: \code{s3-wc-proj-two-decls-ok}, \code{s3-wc-warm-samehead-two-decls-ok}, \code{r2-ptr-alone-ok}, \code{r2-ptr-other-record-order-ok}, \code{r2-ptr-same-record-ok}, \code{r2-ptr-same-record-def-ok}, \code{oh-kept-false-reused-ok}, \code{oh-kept-false-then-true-ok}}\td{An earlier declaration of the file left the full reduction of a projection, or a negative comparison result, in the session; a later declaration asks the cheap question and gets the full answer. Officially every cache lives for one declaration.}\td{The machine itself in a fresh session: without \code{warm}, with the session forgotten before every declaration, and as the strict machine of \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}.}\td{No comparison result, no checked type and no full reduction kept from one declaration to the next. The same change removes 15 of the 74 tolerated accepts and the dependence of the verdict on the schedule. Its price is at most what warm sessions gain, between nothing and 5 \% of one worker's CPU time where that was measured, with an earlier switch set.}}
\tr{\td{Tables keyed by values, with canonical variables: the pair-table class, finding S3-F1}\td{4: \code{s3-wc-canon-fvar-full-first-ok}, \code{r2-id-binder-name-ok}, \code{r2-id-binder-name-def-ok}, \code{r2-id-binder-info-ok}}\td{Inside one declaration. Officially the second lambda has a new variable and an empty cache for its terms, or the accept is an answer of the cache of successful pairs for two terms that differ in a binder name or a binder info only; the machine's memo of the full reduction is keyed by the value under one canonical variable.}\td{The reference engine only. The machine rejects with every switch set, the one without switches included.}\td{The official caches themselves: keyed by terms, per declaration, with a fresh variable per binder instance. No design exists and the cost was never measured. A cheaper rule, which decides a comparison for the stuck projection and for its full reduction and declines when the two verdicts differ, would turn the rejection into a decline, not into the official accept.}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{How they are kept from growing.} Each of the twelve rows carries the mark \code{wrong-reject} in the manifest of the hostile suite, with its cause. \src{scripts/gate.sh} prints each by name on every run and carries a ceiling of 12 that may only go down: a thirteenth fails the gate, and a marked row that gets the official verdict is reported so that its mark and the ceiling go. See [the gate](design-gate-hostile).}
\li{\strong{The way back for one file.} \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} gives the official accept on all twelve, eight through the strict machine and four through the reference engine.}
\li{\strong{The schedule.} The eight rows of the first cause are rejected when one session checks the file and accepted when every declaration has a fresh session, so their verdict depends on the worker count and the session cap, see [the driver's invariants](design-driver-invariants).}
\li{\strong{Not established.} The list is complete for the 1,274 rows of the suite and not beyond it. Other hand-made files of the two classes will be rejected too, and a third family, a let variable used after the end of its let, is known outside the suite with at least one such file; it and the rest of what the alignment has to do are in [the work list](prob-HC9J). The analysis of the twelve is in [the census](rep-MO-1), section 5.2.}
\li{\strong{Found since, outside the suite} (state of 2026-11-26). Ten more hand-made exports that the official checker accepts are rejected by the published default configuration; none is real input, and each was found by a package or a review that was looking for it. Five are the K rule on a major premise that is typed through a let variable, among them the family named in the item above: the machine keeps the declared type after the end of the let, or does not see a let variable that is applied by an over-application (findings MO2-F1 and MO2-F5 of [the package on that rule](rep-MO-2)); a patch that gives the official verdict on them exists and is not applied, because it has not passed a review by another author. Four are the K rule on a major premise that is a beta-redex without a let, or a let term (findings MO2-F3 and MO2-F6 of the same report): the machine types the value where the official kernel types the term; no repair is built. One is a pair that is compared first under \code{eagerReduce} and then in plain mode in one binder, with a \code{Nat.gcd} whose declared body does not compute: the deferred order of the switch \code{eager} runs the plain comparison first ([the review of the eager reduction in place](rep-RV-3A)). They are not rows of the suite yet. They become rows when the change of their area is integrated; a row that is still rejected then gets the mark \code{wrong-reject}, and the ceiling of the gate is raised by that number, which records what is known and not a new defect.}
}
}

\subtree[accepted-risks-declines]{
\title{Declines by design}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A decline is not a wrong verdict.} RLVM answers accept, reject or decline. Input that the pipeline cannot decide with the official verdict is declined: among others a declaration placed after its user in a large file, a missing recursor record, a constant the kernel would generate under a foreign name, metadata inside an inductive record. On the four mutant corpora the reference pipeline declined 1,683 of 17,775 files, 1,197 of them in the corpus that wraps expressions in metadata, with no wrong verdict. With the launcher's re-check of rejected files with metadata the count was 2,034: 351 correct rejections of mutants with a metadata record were reported as declines, the accepted price of [closing the metadata class](dec-7UAL). Since 2026-11-25 a plain run no longer makes that re-check and the count is 1,683 again; 2,034 is the count with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} and with the reference engine as primary engine.}
\li{\strong{Two official non-terminations are declined.} The two arena tests on which the official checker does not terminate are declined within seconds by a bound on the work of one unit.}
}
}

\subtree[accepted-risks-journal]{
\title{The journal itself}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Forester compiles from source.} Forester is not in nixpkgs, so the first build on a machine compiles Forester and its OCaml libraries, which takes several minutes. Later builds reuse the local store.}
\li{\strong{The first two days were written up afterwards.} The trees dated 2026-11-23 and 2026-11-24 were written on 2026-11-24 from the documents under \code{docs/}, the commit history and the status logs of the team, not during the work. Their dates are the dates of the work, in the time zone of the commit history, as far as the documents and logs allow them to be told; the notes given on the first evening are dated 2026-11-23 on that basis. The session trees of that period describe phases of a team of agents, not single sessions.}
\li{\strong{Documents stay whole, inside the journal.} As first decided, the research, design and review documents under \code{docs/} stayed in the repository and a tree stated one point of a document and linked to it. The maintainer's rule of 2026-11-24 supersedes the first half: every note lives in the journal, and the documents are moved in as trees of the kinds Study and Report and as full design documents, each as one tree with its text unchanged; see [standing policies](standing-policies). What stays accepted is the division of work: a short tree states one point and links to the long tree that carries the detail, and the long trees are not rewritten. A moved tree keeps the state, the UTC times and the names of working files of the day it was written.}
\li{\strong{Links point at branch \code{dev}.} A link to a file of the repository shows the file as it is on branch \code{dev} today, which can be later than the state a tree describes. A tree that depends on an exact state names the commit.}
\li{\strong{Two documents were regenerated after the trees were written.} The benchmark tables and the platform document were rewritten on the evening of 2026-11-24 (commit \commit{5bd53c6}), with new measurements of the published defaults. A number that a tree dated 2026-11-23 or 2026-11-24 quotes from them is from the earlier state: \srcat{e021aef}{docs/BENCHMARKS.md} and \srcat{88466b3}{docs/PLATFORM.md}. The trees were not rewritten to the new tables.}
\li{\strong{Working files are not cited.} The team's status logs and scratch directories under \code{work/} are not part of the repository. A number that exists only there is stated in the tree without a link.}
}
}
